  "poisson_disk_procedure_as_asked": {
    "asked_by": "review lane, 2026-09-20T04:40Z, as the condition for closing the 0.0 claim",
    "generator": "open3d.geometry.TriangleMesh.sample_points_poisson_disk (Open3D's own elimination sampler, not hand-rolled blue noise)",
    "radius": "not passed; the sampler derives the elimination radius from number_of_points and mesh surface area",
    "number_of_points": 60000,
    "oversampling": "init_factor=3, so 3x the target is drawn uniformly then eliminated down",
    "measured_evenness_max_over_median_nn": {
      "icosphere/poisson_disk": 1.7,
      "icosphere/uniform_random": 4.19,
      "armadillo/poisson_disk": 1.66,
      "armadillo/uniform_random": 3.99
    },
    "why_their_reproduction_failed": "Their weighted-elimination set measured max/median 4.50 against 3.76 for uniform random, so it was less even than the baseline and could not test the claim. Open3D's sampler measures 1.70 against 4.19 on the same geometry, a 2.5-fold improvement in evenness, which is the input the claim depends on. They were right to withhold their result rather than report it as a contradiction.",
    "claim_reconfirmed_independently_of_the_first_run": "evidence/open3d/poisson-procedure-and-overcall-audit.json: at a voxel of twice the median spacing a zero-error reconstruction reports exactly 0.00000 unsupported area under Poisson-disk sampling against 0.16061 (icosphere) and 0.14497 (armadillo) under uniform random. At one times the spacing it is 0.02695 against 0.85509.",
    "consistent_with_their_own_sweep": "Their corrected table shows both schemes falling together as the support radius grows, with the radius dominating in their setup. That is not in tension with this: their weak blue noise was not more even than uniform random, so their two columns were effectively the same sampling scheme measured twice, and only the radius varied."
  }
